    >>> indices_2D
    (Array([0, 1, 1], dtype=int32), Array([1, 0, 2], dtype=int32))
    >>> print(x_2D[indices_2D])
    [3. 5. 7.]

    The inverse function, ``ravel_multi_index``, can be used to obtain the
    original indices:

    >>> jnp.ravel_multi_index(indices_2D, shape)
    Array([1, 3, 5], dtype=int32)

